from PySide6.QtCore import Qt
from PySide6.QtWidgets import *
TOPICS={
'Persistent left application menu': 'When the horizontal top bar is off, the left app menu stays visible like a traditional GNUstep menu. It follows the active built-in app and contains Info, File, Edit, View, Go, Tools, Windows, Services, Help, Hide, Hide Others, Show All, Print and Quit Preview. It reuses the same app commands as the horizontal menu bar. Preferences → Menu bar layout → Left app menu (no top bar), or the desktop command Use left app menu, selects it. The option Show left app menu when top bar is off is enabled by default. Layout and visibility save and back up automatically. Reserved space prevents windows and docks from covering it.',
'Choose top or left menus': 'Classic app menu bar and Desktop toolbar are horizontal layouts. Left app menu (no top bar) removes the horizontal bar and shows the persistent left panel. Ctrl+Alt+B toggles between top bar and left-menu layout; Ctrl+Alt+M restores horizontal Classic menus. Start with --left-menu to force the left layout, or bash run-left-menu.sh from source. Clip, docks, pins, and their fitting behavior remain available. Quit Preview closes the app with normal document checks; host logout is separate.',
'Restore File and Edit menus': 'Version 0.15.1 uses explicit embedded menu buttons and restores Classic menus on its first launch. Press Ctrl+Alt+M, choose Show File / Edit Menus on the desktop right-click menu, or click Show File / Edit Menus Now in Preferences. The separate MacaqueSTEP 0.15.1 — Classic Menus launcher and --classic-menus command force this layout even when Hidden was saved. Close an existing older preview before starting the repair launcher. --version and --diagnose identify the running code and saved menu settings.',
'Classic application menus': 'The new default bar has MacaqueSTEP, File, Edit, View, Go, Window, and Help. Menus follow the active built-in app and retain the last focused text/file control. Editor Open, Save, Save As, Find and close retain existing safeguards. File Viewer gets file clipboard operations, New Folder, Rename, Trash and Get Info. Notes get save/export/delete; Paint gets PNG saving, undo/color/clear; Terminal gets Copy and explicit PTY Paste. Unsupported commands are disabled. Text views, Notes and Paint support Page Setup and Print with confirmation; PDF output was tested. External apps keep their own menus.',
'Menu bar layout choices': 'Preferences → Menu bar layout selects Classic app menu bar, Desktop toolbar, or Hidden. View and desktop right-click menus offer the same choices. Ctrl+Alt+B hides/restores the last visible layout. The initial 0.15 choice is Classic; subsequent choices persist and are backed up. Clip, docks, icon themes, pinning, and extra-column fitting remain available.',
'Traditional desktop layout': 'Turn off Show top bar in Preferences for a traditional Window Maker-style desktop. Right-click empty desktop space to access Applications, Preferences, Commands, Workspaces, Windows, and Drawers. Restore the bar with Show top bar in that menu or Ctrl+Alt+B. The setting is saved and included in backups. Ctrl+Space, Ctrl+Alt+P, and Clip workspace arrows work while the bar is hidden. Host panels and the outer application title bar are separate; F11 still toggles full screen.',
'Pin and Unpin applications': 'Right-click an app in Applications, the side dock, Clip, Drawer Shelf, running dock, or a window title bar. Choose Pin to side dock or Pin to running-app dock. Already pinned apps offer Unpin instead. The destinations are independent. Bottom pins have a small gold marker and stay after closing the app; click to launch. Unpinning never closes windows. An unpinned running app keeps its ordinary task tile; a closed unpinned app disappears. Repeated Pin actions do not duplicate launchers. Pins are saved and backed up. Pinned document apps reopen the tool rather than unsaved document contents.',
'Bottom dock pins and running apps': 'Dropping onto the bottom dock now pins a launcher without launching it or removing the source. Persistent pins remain visible across all workspaces and merge their matching running windows into one tile, including when ordinary tasks use per-window grouping. Count badges and window choosers handle multiple windows. Current-workspace-only mode still filters unpinned tasks. Explicit custom host commands can be pinned, but their external windows are not tracked.',
'Classic icons and dock sizing': 'Classic now has a separate original pixel-style app icon set. Light and Dark retain their modern icons. Change Appearance in Preferences; dock icons and About update immediately. Dock tile size ranges from 24 to 68 pixels. Fitting uses extra columns so the slider changes the actual size. Matching running tiles, Clip tiles, and the Drawer Shelf update too; large Clip collections may reduce their own size to fit.',
'Window Maker behavior': 'Right-click a title bar or running tile for Hide application, Hide other applications, Show application, Show application here, and Show all applications. Hiding preserves app windows and unsaved contents separately from minimization. Dock launchers restore hidden apps. Wheel up/down on a title bar shades/unshades. Ctrl-double-click maximizes height, Shift-double-click width, both keys both axes; a modified double-click restores previous geometry. Preferences optionally enables double-click launching for dock icons. See WINDOW-MAKER-BEHAVIOR.md for links to the official guide and remaining differences.',
'Installing MacaqueSTEP': 'The supplied .deb installs the preview app on Debian/Ubuntu with supported Qt 6.8+ Python packages. Use sudo apt install ./macaquestep-preview_0.23_all.deb, then open MacaqueSTEP Preview from the menu. Alternatively bash install.sh makes a managed per-user installation with a private runtime. bash install.sh --uninstall removes that user installation and keeps Notes, Tasks and preferences. These are application installers; bootable OS installation and external-window session integration are unfinished.',
'Workspace Clip and draggable docks': 'The paperclip at top left is a workspace-specific launcher dock. Arrows change workspace; click the paperclip to collapse or expand icons. Right-click it to add a launcher or rename the workspace. Drag launcher icons between the side dock, Clip, and graphical Drawer Shelf. Normal drag moves, Ctrl-drop copies. Drop on an icon to insert before it, or on empty dock space to append. Drag running tiles to pin a launcher; running tiles remain until their windows close. Drop a launcher on the running dock to pin it without launching or removing its source. Arbitrary host desktop file/URL drops are not supported. Settings save automatically and backups include them.',
'Fit side dock without scrolling': 'Preferences offers Fit side-dock icons without scrolling, enabled by default. The chosen 24–68 pixel tile size is preserved using extra columns if necessary. Running tiles match the effective size when matching is enabled. Disable fitting to use the chosen size with scrolling. Dock position still selects Left or Right. Preferences can hide/show the Clip or restore default side launchers. Right-click side/Clip icons for removal and transfer actions. The graphical Drawer Shelf supports icon dragging; the top-bar drawer menu is for launching.',
'Desktop sessions': 'Version 0.11 saves up to 20 named window layouts. Open Desktop Sessions with Ctrl+Alt+L, Applications, or Settings. Save positions, workspace names, and window states; loading leaves other windows and unsaved documents open. Optionally open missing Files, Notes, Tasks, Calculator, Clock, Help, Search, Settings, Sounds, and About tools. Documents, terminal processes, media, and host commands are never reopened by sessions. Select a startup layout or Default startup. Undo layout (Ctrl+Alt+Z) restores the preceding arrangement without closing newly opened tools.',
'Commands and shortcuts': 'Commands in the top bar or Ctrl+Alt+P searches apps, open windows, workspaces, and layout actions. Enter activates a result. Keyboard Shortcuts in Settings changes desktop bindings. Clear disables one; Apply validates and saves; Restore defaults loads defaults into the editor. Conflicting bindings are rejected. Host shortcuts may take precedence. Ctrl+Alt+H shows the desktop and restores the minimized group on the next press. Tiling and cascading affect visible windows in this workspace, including sticky windows. Help shows default keys, not customized bindings.',
'Unix readiness': 'Unix Readiness Inventory lists the actual host, reported sysconf values, and presence of sampled command-line utilities. It can export JSON. Detected commands and reported versions do not establish behavioral conformance. The full OS has not been certified or fully tested. POSIX.1-2024 and SUS Version 5 are a proposed engineering target; a complete requirements audit, behavioral test suite, fixes, documentation, and installed-image validation remain.',
'Window Maker-style controls': 'Version 0.10 adds title-bar shading, sticky windows on all workspaces, keep-above ordering, optional focus follows mouse, named workspaces, saved per-app rules, and customizable launcher drawers. Right-click a title bar or running tile for window actions. Double-click a title bar to shade; Preferences can change this to Maximize. Right-click a workspace button to rename it. Window Rules saves starting workspace and window states for internal apps. Drawers contains editable groups of built-in tools or explicit host commands. External windows remain managed by the host desktop.',
'Launcher drawers': 'Open Drawers in the top bar or side dock. Customize drawers lets you add, rename, or remove groups, add built-in tools or a host command, and reorder entries. Commands are parsed as a program plus arguments without shell interpretation. Quote arguments containing spaces. Shell pipelines and redirects are not interpreted. Saved drawers are included in preference backups.',
'Sounds and About': 'Version 0.9 adds 12 original retro desktop effects. Open Sound Effects through Applications, Settings Hub, Preferences, or About. Set the effects volume, mute them, enable categories, or preview sounds. This does not change host output volume. About MacaqueSTEP now has Overview, Displays, Storage, and Support tabs, with real host-reported details and Made by Drew attribution. Settings Hub links to the controls available in the preview; display information is read-only and hardware controls remain host-dependent.',
'Completion pass in 0.8': 'File Viewer now supports multi-selection, folder/symlink copying, copy/cut/paste using file URLs, cancellation, and same-filesystem moves. File Search recursively searches names with cancellation and a 1,000-result limit. Installed Host Apps discovers desktop entries and launches external applications using gio. System Controls detects supported host audio and brightness utilities and reads Linux battery status; network/Bluetooth buttons open detected host settings apps. Backups packages Notes, Tasks, and settings; restore is validated and applied on the next launch, retaining a recovery database. Project Status lists the remaining bootable-OS work. Ctrl+Alt+F opens File Search.',
'Square docks and snapping': 'The bottom dock now uses individual square icon tiles matching the side dock, with wallpaper visible in the gaps. App names are available as tooltips and accessibility labels. Grouped apps have a count badge. Tile size follows the side dock by default; disable matching in Preferences for independent sizing. Scroll the bottom tiles with the wheel when they overflow. Right-click a tile for window actions and snapping. Right-click a title bar to snap to halves or quarters and restore the previous geometry. Ctrl+Alt+Shift plus an arrow snaps the active window to a half. Overview in the top bar, or Ctrl+Alt+O, shows all four workspaces; double-click a window to activate it.',
'Running-app dock': 'The bottom dock uses floating individual square tiles for open MacaqueSTEP windows. The side dock contains pinned launchers. Click a single-window tile to activate it or minimize the active window. A minimized app stays in the bottom dock; click to restore it. Apps on another workspace bring you to that workspace. Grouped multi-window tiles open a window chooser. Right-click for Restore, Minimize, Maximize, Move to Workspace, or Close. Preferences controls visibility, grouping, workspace scope, and icon size. Scroll horizontally when many windows are open. The dock tracks the preview’s internal windows.',
'Welcome to 0.23': 'MacaqueSTEP 0.23 includes four workspaces, an interactive terminal, Paint, Archive Viewer, and this Help center. Use Ctrl+Space to search all desktop tools. This remains a native desktop preview on your existing computer; it is not a bootable operating system.',
'Workspaces': 'Use the four numbered buttons in the top bar to switch workspaces. Ctrl+Alt+1 through Ctrl+Alt+4 also switch. Right-click a window title bar to move it to another workspace. Apps remain running when hidden. Opening an existing app from the dock takes you to its workspace. Open Windows lists all workspaces.',
'Interactive terminal': 'The terminal now runs a real shell through a pseudo-terminal. Normal shell history, completion, Ctrl+C, Ctrl+D, and interactive prompts work. Ctrl+Shift+C copies selected text; Ctrl+Shift+V pastes. Shift+PageUp and Shift+PageDown browse output history. Text is rendered in monochrome. Advanced alternate-screen and mouse-reporting programs may differ from a full terminal emulator. Commands run on your actual computer as your user. Closing Terminal ends its shell session. Never paste commands you do not intend to run.',
'Paint': 'Choose Brush, Eraser, Line, Rectangle, or Ellipse. Set the color and brush size, then draw with the left mouse button. Undo keeps eight image states. Open supports images up to 16 megapixels. Save PNG writes a PNG file. The canvas scrolls when larger than the window. Close asks before discarding unsaved changes.',
'Archive Viewer': 'Browse ZIP and TAR variants up to 256 MB and 10,000 entries. Preview UTF-8 text files up to 1 MB. Extract selected regular files up to 16 MB to a chosen directory. Selected files are flattened to their base names, links are blocked, and existing files are never overwritten. This version does not extract entire directories.',
'Files and documents': 'File Viewer browses actual host files. It can copy selected files/folders in the background, cut and paste within a filesystem, rename, create folders, and move selected items to the host Trash. Copy refuses to overwrite an existing destination. Restore trashed files through your host file manager. Text Editor supports Save, Save As, Find, atomic saving, and unsaved-change prompts.',
'Notes and tasks': 'Notes and tasks save in a local SQLite database under $XDG_DATA_HOME/MacaqueSTEP/preview (normally ~/.local/share/MacaqueSTEP/preview). Data carries over from version 0.4. Notes flush pending edits on close. Task changes save immediately. Back up organizer.sqlite3 while the preview is closed.',
'Appearance and dock': 'Preferences controls Classic, Light, and Dark themes, custom wallpaper, dock size and side, and startup window positions. Scroll the dock if icons are below the window. Workspaces share the same wallpaper and theme. F11 toggles full screen; Escape returns to a window.',
'Keyboard shortcuts': 'F11: full screen. Escape: windowed. Ctrl+Space: Applications. Ctrl+Alt+T: Terminal. Ctrl+Alt+W: Open Windows. Ctrl+Alt+1–4: switch workspace. Ctrl+Alt+S: shade active window. Ctrl+Alt+D: customize drawers. Ctrl+Alt+PageUp/PageDown: cycle workspaces. Ctrl+Tab: next internal window. In Text Editor: Ctrl+S Save, Ctrl+O Open, Ctrl+F Find. In Terminal: Ctrl+Shift+C/V copy/paste.',
'Media and system': 'Media Player opens local audio/video with Play, Pause, Stop, seek, and volume. Playback depends on host codecs and audio devices. System Information reports actual host details. Clock & Timer works only while the preview runs. Other unhandled file types may open in external host applications.',
'Operating system status': 'The desktop currently runs as an application. It does not install a login session, manage external application windows, or contain a FreeBSD kernel. A bootable Unix image and hardware support are separate future stages. No UNIX certification is claimed.'}
class Help(QWidget):
 def __init__(self):
  super().__init__();v=QVBoxLayout(self);search=QLineEdit();search.setPlaceholderText('Search Help…');v.addWidget(search);split=QSplitter();items=QListWidget();text=QTextBrowser();split.addWidget(items);split.addWidget(text);split.setStretchFactor(1,1);v.addWidget(split,1)
  for title in TOPICS:items.addItem(title)
  items.currentTextChanged.connect(lambda title:text.setPlainText(title+'\n\n'+TOPICS.get(title,'')))
  def filter_topics(s):
   for i in range(items.count()):item=items.item(i);item.setHidden(s.lower() not in (item.text()+' '+TOPICS[item.text()]).lower())
  search.textChanged.connect(filter_topics);items.setCurrentRow(0)
